WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:10.490
As technology continues to intermingle with
our lives and the world’s dependence on

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.019
fossil fuels dwindles, the humble battery
has grown in importance.

00:00:15.019 --> 00:00:19.150
The market for lithium ion batteries, the
most popular battery type, has grown by an

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:25.210
average of 21% every year since 2004, and
that is expected to accelerate even further

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:30.630
as Tesla and Panasonic’s joint venture,
the Gigafactory, a massive lithium ion battery

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:35.850
factory that will double current output, begins
to ramp up production and as renewable energy

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.579
continues to get cheaper.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:42.350
So are we moving towards a future where the
entire planet is battery-powered?

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:47.190
There are two big factors that will determine
that possibility: 1) how light and energy

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.420
dense we can make batteries, and 2) whether
we’ll even be able to physically manufacture

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.420
enough batteries.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.780
This video covers part 2 of this question,
and Henry of MinutePhysics is covering part

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.310
1 – I’ll link to his video at the end.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.920
When asked how many gigafactories the world
would need to transition the whole world to

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.030
sustainable energy, this was Elon Musk’s
response.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.000
‘Ah, it’s about one hundred roughly.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.490
It’s not ten it’s a thousand, most likely
a hundred”

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:18.580
Let’s investigate how much truth there is
to that statement.Before we deep dive into

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:19.580
this question.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:24.590
Let’s first explore how much energy a gigawatt
hour, the main unit of energy we will be using

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.870
in this video, really contains.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:32.320
A single battery with 1 Gigawatt hour of storage
could power a single 100 Watt bulb for 1141

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.320
years.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:40.470
It would allow the Tesla Model S 100D drive
to the moon AND back 8 times or it could power

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.650
New York city at peak summer electricity usage
for 270 seconds.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:45.650
[1]

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:50.270
The Gigafactory in Nevada is expected to manufacture
35 gigawatt hours of battery cells a year

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:54.380
by 2020, enough to supply half a million vehicles
a year.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:02:01.230
That’s pretty impressive, but last year
72.1 million cars were built along with 22.5

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:06.010
million commercial vehicles, consisting mostly
of large trucks and buses [3][4].

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:10.869
How many gigafactories would we need to replace
current fossil fuel car manufacturing with

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:12.280
all electric cars.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.849
Let’s assume each car would use the same
battery as the Tesla Model 3, we do not have

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:21.569
a confirmed battery for the Model 3 yet, but
Musk has stated it will be between 60 and

00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:24.880
70 kWh, so let’s go with 65 kWh.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.439
Even with Tesla’s smallest planned battery,
we would need 134 Gigafactories to produce

00:02:30.439 --> 00:02:34.120
72.1 million Model 3s per year.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.579
And this isn’t even counting the 22.5 million
commercial vehicles produced each year.

00:02:39.579 --> 00:02:44.230
I’m not convinced that a Tesla Truck is
really feasible with current battery densities

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:51.680
since the 3000kWh of battery power necessary
would take about 20 tonnes, but we’re not

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.459
at the theoretical limit yet, Brian!

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:57.120
If battery technology continues to improve,
something like a lithium-sulfur battery of

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.239
that capacity could be as light as 6 tonnes
but regardless in order to build enough 3000

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:08.769
kWh batteries, we would need another 20 gigafactories
just to replace the quarter of a million trucks

00:03:08.769 --> 00:03:10.840
[5] being built in North America alone.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:16.559
[6] That’s not even including the other
22.25 million commercial vehicles in theworld,

00:03:16.559 --> 00:03:20.819
which would likely bring the total required
Gigafactories to replace current automotive

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:23.650
production to around 300.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:28.950
So just replacing automotive production alone
would be a monumental task that Tesla simply

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:34.430
cannot take on alone and Musk’s estimate
of 100 gigafactories is a bit inaccurate with

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:40.290
current production targets, but Musk did state
during the 2016 Tesla shareholder meeting

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.260
that they could potentially triple the planned
output of the Gigafactory, if the demand was

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.260
there.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.919
If that is true, 100 gigafactories seems fairly
accurate.

00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:54.889
Tesla has equally grand ambitions of providing
battery storage for the grid and home too

00:03:54.889 --> 00:03:59.809
and have allocated production to the tune
of 50 gigawatt hours a year to run alongside

00:03:59.809 --> 00:04:03.419
the 35 gigawatt hour cell production for this
purpose.

00:04:03.419 --> 00:04:09.349
Let’s analyse how many Gigafactories the
grid would need to become stable under 100%

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:14.249
renewable energy, keeping in mind that we
do not need to produce all of this in 1 year.

00:04:14.249 --> 00:04:19.680
The European Renewable Energy Council optimistically
targeted 2050 as the year that Europe could

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.960
generate 100% of it’s energy with renewable
energy.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.940
So let’s say we have 33 years to produce
the required storage.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:34.030
That statistic of a 1 gWh battery supporting
New York for 270 seconds should give you an

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:38.770
idea of how much battery storage will be needed
to support a city, but not all electricity

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:43.590
will come from battery, much of it will come
directly from the power source as it is being

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:44.590
generated.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.460
There really is no reason why we could not
rely completely on renewable energy supported

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:54.650
by batteries and pumped storage, and the european
renewable energy council has suggested this

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:58.389
breakdown of energy sources to reach that
goal by 2050.

00:04:58.389 --> 00:05:04.150
Let’s first look at the suggested wind power
capacity of 462 GigaWatts.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:08.211
We can estimate how much storage this would
need by taking a look at Australia’s new

00:05:08.211 --> 00:05:13.490
Hornsdale Wind Farm, which is supported by
the world’s largest battery storage facility,

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:18.740
consisting of 129 MWh of storage provided
by the Tesla Powerpack.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:24.449
This storage is there to allow the 99 wind
turbines with a capacity of 315 MegaWatts

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:26.729
to provide stable power to the grid.

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:29.830
Removing the problems of variable wind speeds.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:38.889
Scaling that 315 MegaWatts to 462 GigaWatts
would require 1467 times more storage, or

00:05:38.889 --> 00:05:41.409
189.2 GWh of storage.

00:05:41.409 --> 00:05:45.520
A single Gigafactory could produce that in
less than 4 years.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.720
Hydro, Biomass, Concentrated Solar Power and
Geothermal would require no battery storage

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.410
as they are relatively stable over the course
of a day.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:56.509
Ocean energy would require similar storage
to wind.

00:05:56.509 --> 00:06:01.169
Concentrated Solar Power can stores its energy
in molten salt and Geothermal in particular

00:06:01.169 --> 00:06:05.229
has an incredibly stable capacity factor,
even beating coal.

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:10.660
The capacity factor tells us an energy sources
ability to deliver on it’s maximum possible

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:11.699
output.

00:06:11.699 --> 00:06:15.620
Solar has one of the lowest capacity factors
over the course of a single day, because it

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:21.030
generates 0% of it’s potential power at
night, and thus it requires the most energy

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.030
storage.

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:26.000
As a case study for how much storage we would
need here, we can take Tesla’s project in

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.360
Hawaii to provide the isolated community of
Kauai with a solar powered microgrid.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:37.020
The Kauai project consists of a 13 megawatt
solar farm paired with a 52 megawatt-hour

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:38.520
battery installation.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.750
Already we can see that the ratio of batteries
to power capacity is MUCH higher, and this

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:47.020
on a tropical island in the pacific ocean.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:51.280
Europe would likely need higher capacity to
make up for less sun, which would likely reduce

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.680
the ratio of capacity to storage.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.440
This may skew results.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:02.499
Nevertheless scaling that 13 megawatt solar
farm to 962 Gigawatts would require 74,000

00:07:02.499 --> 00:07:08.219
times more storage or 3848 Gigawatt-hours
of storage.

00:07:08.219 --> 00:07:11.770
Adding in our storage needs for Wind (189.2
GWh) and Ocean (26.6 GWh), we get a total

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:16.040
of 4064 GWh for Europe alone.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:20.659
Europe currently makes up about 13.3% [8]
of the world’s energy demand.

00:07:20.659 --> 00:07:27.509
So an estimate, full of assumptions, for the
battery storage the world would need is 30556

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:28.509
Gigawatt hours.

00:07:28.509 --> 00:07:33.840
That much battery storage would allow New
York to run at peak usage for 95 91 days straight,

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:40.080
without any additional power, which would
take 18.5 gigafactories 33 years to manufacture

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.749
and with current prices per (124$)/kWh of
storage would cost 3.8 trillion dollars.

00:07:45.749 --> 00:07:50.180
This lines up neatly with Tesla’s goal to
build 10-20 gigafactories.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:55.860
With 18 gigafactories running at current production
targets, Tesla would be able to produce enough

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:02.150
cars to overtake Ford’s current market share
in motor vehicles with 9 million vehicles

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:06.759
AND allow Tesla to produce enough battery
storage to help transition the world to a

00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:11.310
100% renewable grid over the next 33 year.

